// Generated by native/tools/make_atom_learn.js from index.html (SIMS['Atom Lab'].changelog). Do not edit by hand.
{"7.7.3", "Remembers Less info / More info", "The Less info / More info button on the picture is now remembered. Pick one and it stays that way when you close and reopen Physics Lab, and when you change element. Before, it always started with whatever suited the screen size, which on a phone showed too much."},
{"7.7.2", "The nucleon you dive into stays in focus", "In the quark view, the depth-of-field look focused on the front of the nucleus. If you tapped a nucleon a bit deeper inside, its quarks came out blurred and without their u and d letters. Now the camera focuses on the nucleon you dived into: it is always sharp, with its letters, and it always shows its quarks, even in big nuclei where only the 45 nearest nucleons get quarks. Nucleons in front of it stay sharp, deeper ones blur."},
{"7.7.1", "Cloud no longer slows other windows", "Zoomed out, the electron cloud was drawn as about 10,000 separate little squares every frame. On a fast monitor that flooded the browser’s shared graphics process, so videos and streams in other windows could stutter or stop. Now all the dots go to the graphics chip in one go (WebGL), one draw instead of thousands, and the cloud looks the same. Zoomed in, with only a few dots on screen, the lab still draws them the old way, which is cheaper there. Without WebGL it falls back to the old way everywhere."},
{"7.7", "Phase colours and every orbital", "The Cloud, Cloud 3D and Slice views now colour the electron’s wave by its sign: the + half in the accent colour, the − half in orange. Where the two meet, the wave is zero and the electron is never found (a node), so the empty shells inside 2s, 3s and 4s and the flat node planes of p, d and f are easy to see. New Orientation setting: px, py and pz, all five d orbitals and all seven f orbitals. New orbitals 4s, 4p, 4d and 4f. Every shape comes from the exact hydrogen-like formula (radial Laguerre polynomial times the real angular shape chemists draw). Photon jumps follow the dipole rule for orientations too: light can only connect orientations whose overlap with x, y or z is not zero, so the cloud lands on a real, allowed shape."},
{"7.6", "Faster, sharper quark view", "Zooming into the quarks is several times smoother, most of all in big nuclei like uranium. The soft glow behind every proton and neutron is now painted once at a lower resolution (a blur has no fine detail, so nothing is lost), and only the sharp parts are drawn at full resolution. New depth-of-field look, like a camera focused on the front of the nucleus: the front nucleons’ quarks are sharp, the deeper ones are soft and faded, and their u / d letters are hidden. In Cinematic style the whole nucleus now sits on one dark backdrop, which also fixes a white glare that could appear on the light theme. Hard balls style gets the same focus effect."},
{"7.5", "Light spectrum, decay history, half-lives in the table", "New Light spectrum card: every line the atom on screen can give off, from the ultraviolet to the infrared, grouped in their series (for hydrogen: Lyman, Balmer, Paschen, Brackett and Pfund), with a to-scale strip of the visible part in the real colours. For hydrogen this is its real spectrum; for other elements it is the spectrum of that element with only one electron left, which is what this lab shows (a real ion, seen in hot plasmas and stars), and the card says so. Lines your photon jumps made are marked. The wavelengths now include the nucleus’s real mass (the electron and nucleus both circle their common centre), so hydrogen’s Hα is 656.47 nm instead of 656.1 nm, and photon jumps use the same numbers. Not included, and stated on the card: fine structure, the small splitting of each line by relativity, which grows large for heavy ions. New Decay history card: the whole chain since you last picked a nucleus, each step with how it decayed and how long it lived, and the total time. The periodic table now shows the half-life and decay types of the nucleus on screen (under the table), and of each element’s usual isotope (hover over a square)."},
{"7", "Real radioactive decay", "Unstable nuclei now decay on their own, all the time, just as real ones do. Each one gets a random lifetime from its real half-life (the chance of decaying is the same every moment, whether you watch or not), and when it decays you see what flies out: an α particle, an electron and an antineutrino (β⁻), a positron and a neutrino (β⁺), neutrons or protons, a small cluster like carbon-14, or two fragments in a fission. The lab then follows the new nucleus, so decay chains go on by themselves: build oganesson-294 and within a fraction of a second it becomes livermorium, then flerovium, which may split in two. The readout shows the half-life, the ways it can decay with their real odds, and the chance it decays in the next minute (uranium-238: about 3×10⁻¹⁴ %). Half-lives and decay odds for 3,306 unstable nuclei come from NUBASE2020. Pausing stops the clocks, and so does holding a slider (so dragging through the isotopes does not set off decays). Very short-lived steps in a chain are shown one per frame so you can see them, but the times written are the real ones. After a fission, or a decay into something this lab cannot build, tap ☢ Build another to watch a fresh one. New achievement: Half-Life."},
{"6.7", "Help guide on one page, with a jump bar", "The “What am I even looking at?” guide is now one long page instead of dropdown boxes: every section is open, the headings stay as headings, and a row of tappable headings at the top jumps you to any section. Each heading also has a small ↑ that takes you back to that row. The text itself is unchanged."},
{"6.6", "Bug fix: view buttons stay put", "Changing the Electron picture (Cloud, Cloud 3D, Slice) could make the whole Electron card disappear, so you could not switch back. The card now stays, whatever picture you choose."},
{"6.5", "Bohr 1913 style", "New Orbit style setting (Orbit view only). Bohr 1913 draws the atom as Bohr did: the nucleus is one small ball with a plus sign (not to scale; neutrons were only found in 1932, so none are drawn), the allowed circles n = 1, 2, 3, 4 are drawn as clean rings with the electron’s circle in bold, and the electron is a ball with a minus sign. No quarks, no fuzz, no trail. The ↓ Emit photon and ↑ Absorb photon buttons work as before, and a flash marks where the electron lands after its jump. The readout only says what Bohr knew and the ❓ guide explains the picture. Modern is still the default. Also: the photon label no longer runs off the picture on a phone, and the Orbit view is now called “electron on a circle” to tell it apart from the Bohr style."},
{"6.3", "Element card", "Under the periodic table, the one-line note is now an element card: a tile with the atomic number and symbol, the name, its family, period and group, whether it is a solid, liquid or gas at room temperature, a ☢ flag if it has no stable isotope, and a plain-words line about where you meet it in daily life (Touchscreens for indium, Pepto-Bismol for bismuth, atomic clocks for caesium …). About 50 elements have their own line; the rest get one based on their family. The everyday facts were checked against several sources. The “What is to scale, and what is not” part of the ❓ guide has one more sentence. Read carefully and you may find something in it."},
{"6.2", "Steadier quarks and a sharper info button", "Fixed quarks flickering, disappearing and reappearing when you look closely at a nucleus. Nearby nucleons could swap their front-to-back order from one frame to the next, so one covered its neighbour’s quarks and then uncovered them. The order is now stable, and the choice of which nucleons get quarks no longer flips at the edge of the picture. In big nuclei only the 45 nearest nucleons get quarks, and as the nucleus turned or you moved the picture, nucleons kept passing that limit, so quark triplets appeared and disappeared out of thin air. Now each nucleon’s quarks fade in and out over about a third of a second, and its glow changes with them, so nothing pops. The ⓘ more info / less info button at the bottom left of the picture is now drawn as a proper icon instead of a text character, so it is sharp on every screen. It is also a little bigger and easier to tap, and it follows the text size setting."},
{"6.1", "Every view can be dragged, picture can no longer get lost", "Orbit: drag to turn the atom and see the orbit from other angles (it starts looking straight down, and a Reset view button and little axes appear once you turn it). Cloud: drag to move around at any zoom. Slice: drag up or down to slide the cut through the cloud. In Orbit, Cloud 3D and Slice, Shift-drag, right-drag or the ✥ button moves the picture instead. The Centre button now shows whenever the picture has been moved. If you moved the picture and then zoomed in, the electron cloud (or the nucleus) could end up off screen and the view looked empty. Now the picture always stays within reach at every zoom, and the ⌖ Centre button still brings it back."},
{"6", "What am I even looking at?", "A new ❓ button at the top opens a plain-words guide to the atom on screen: the nucleus, protons and neutrons, the quarks inside them, the electron, why only one electron is drawn, what the current picture (Orbit, Cloud, Cloud 3D or Slice) really shows, what every number at the top means, and what is to scale and what is not. It uses the element you have open, and every section opens and closes."},
{"5.6", "Weight panel", "New ⚖ Weight panel in the Learn card, for the atom you have open. Five tabs: the parts (what protons, neutrons and electrons weigh, and why electrons barely count), missing mass (the parts add up to more than the atom; the difference is the binding energy, E = mc²), how many atoms make up a grain of rice or a person, what is inside a proton (about 99 % of its weight is not quarks) and a mass ladder from the electron to the Sun. Binding energies come from measured masses of all 3,557 known isotopes (mendeleev database, checked against published values for He-4, C-12, Fe-56, U-238 and others); isotopes nobody has weighed use a formula and say so. The Scale and Weight panels now have a bar at the top: ◀ ▶ and a dropdown to change the element, and − / + / usual to change the neutron count. The panel updates at once, and the lab behind it changes too, so you never have to close the panel to compare elements."},
{"5.5", "Temperature", "Every element now has a facts box under the periodic table: melting point, boiling point (in K, °C and °F), its state at room temperature, density, and a bar showing where solid, liquid and gas begin. The ⓘ readout gets a short line too. A new Temperature card has a slider (0 to 6,000 K) with a little box of atoms: locked and jiggling as a solid, flowing as a liquid, flying as a gas. Buttons jump to cold, room temperature, just melted and just boiled. Special cases are explained: carbon and arsenic sublime, helium needs pressure to freeze, phosphorus and sulfur have several forms, and the heaviest elements were never measured. Data: Wikipedia data pages cross-checked with the mendeleev database. The Temperature card has a new “What is temperature, really?” explanation (temperature is the average motion of the atoms; heat, absolute zero, melting and boiling explained simply) and a live line with the typical speed of an atom of the chosen element at the chosen temperature."},
{"5", "Realistic nucleus", "The nucleons are no longer laid out on a tidy spiral. Their positions are now random samples from the real nuclear density (Woods–Saxon, as used in heavy-ion collision simulations), with no overlaps, a neutron skin that grows with the neutron excess, and the measured deformation: uranium-238 and the rare earths are stretched like rugby balls, silicon-28 is flattened, and magic-number nuclei such as lead-208 stay round. Deformation for nuclei without a measurement is an estimate from shell structure, and for the superheavy ones it is a guess. New 🎲 Reshuffle nucleons button: every click is another possible snapshot, since in reality no nucleon has a fixed place. The nucleus label now names the shape. The text at the top of the picture is now short: the essentials plus, when the nucleus is in view, its shape. Everything else (electron chance inside the nucleus, real half-life, relativity numbers, nucleon edge) sits behind a new ⓘ more info button at the bottom left of the picture; wide screens show it by default. The shape label moved from under the nucleus into the readout, so it no longer lands on the electron cloud. Long readout lines now wrap on narrow screens in every lab."},
{"4.5", "Learn panels and a longer timeline", "New Learn card with two panels. 📏 Scale: imagine the nucleus as a pea, marble, tennis ball, football or beach ball, and see how far away the electron would be, with real numbers for the element you have open (plus a powers-of-ten ruler from a hair down to a quark). 🕰 Timeline: nine steps from Democritus to quarks, each with a button that jumps to that model in the lab. The timeline grows from 9 to 22 steps: the periodic table, radioactivity, proof that atoms exist, the proton, electron waves, the Pauli rule, antimatter, fission, moving single atoms, oganesson, the Higgs boson and the first photo of an electron orbital. More ▶ buttons jump to matching atoms in the lab."},
{"4.1", "Save image", "New 📷 Save image button above the picture (works in every lab): saves exactly what you see, readout included, as a PNG. On a phone it opens the share sheet, where you can pick Save to Photos; on a computer it downloads to your Downloads folder."},
{"4", "Realistic quarks", "Quark style: Cinematic, Accurate or Classic. Quarks carry red, green or blue colour charge and swap colours as gluons pass between them; a flickering gluon field replaces the tubes; quarks are points with a soft halo. A mass bar shows quark masses are only about 1% of a proton. Heavy nuclei get a neutron-rich skin."},
{"3.5", "Periodic table and isotopes", "Click any of the 118 elements, hydrogen to oganesson. Real half-lives for every known isotope (3,557). Heavy atoms show X-rays in picometres and a relativity note. Fixed the electron orbit spinning too fast to see around big nuclei. Nucleus card moved to the top, with ± proton and neutron buttons. The side panel is wider, so the table is larger with clearer symbols. Elements with no stable isotope now have a dashed outline instead of faded italic text. A dropdown of 20 well-known nuclei (carbon-14, uranium-235, lead-208, cobalt-60 ...) with why each is famous. The − and + buttons now sit beside the Protons and Neutrons sliders. New Scale setting: Fixed draws every atom at the same scale, so you can watch orbits shrink as the nucleus grows. Changing protons now brings the usual neutron count with it. The Famous isotopes picker shows the isotope you chose. Changing the element keeps your zoom: the picture stays the same size on screen instead of jumping. Changing the isotope no longer drops the nucleon you were following. Once zoomed in, drag to move around the atom, nucleus or nucleon. In the 3D cloud, drag still rotates; Shift-drag, right-drag or the ✥ button moves. New Centre button."},
{"3", "Elements, Slice view and presets", "The nucleus label shows the real nuclide, e.g. Helium-4 (⁴He), and whether it is stable. Cross-section of the cloud, coloured by the wave's sign. Choose the cut direction and position. One-click nuclei (hydrogen-1, deuterium, helium-4, lithium-7). Settings survive switching labs and reloading. Defaults button. Protons 1–10, neutrons 0–12, real half-lives for every nucleus, X-ray photons for heavy nuclei, Carbon-12 / Oxygen-16 / Neon-20 presets. Each lab now shows its own update number. Small changes get a decimal. Click the Update badge to see this list."},
{"2.6*", "Cleaner layout and names", "Top bar decluttered; controls grouped into side cards. Dropdown options now say what they are, e.g. \"Orbit (Bohr model)\". Page scrolls over the canvas on phones; readout text wraps on narrow screens."},
{"2.5*", "Photon jumps", "Electron jumps between levels with emitted / absorbed photons and selection rules; the cloud morphs. Optional automatic emit / absorb. Every photon leaves a coloured line on a wavelength strip. Photon travels all the way to the canvas edge; wave-packet fix."},
{"2*", "Zoom to the nucleus and the quarks", "Zoom in to find the nucleus, with a scale bar. Atom Lab opens first; bug fixes and UI cleanup. Removed stray dots along the canvas edges. One continuous zoom from the electron cloud down to quarks and gluons. Nucleon edges follow the real charge density instead of a hard surface. Scale bar with powers of ten (1 fm = 10⁻¹⁵ m)."},
{"1.5*", "Probability cloud and 3D cloud", "Electron shown as a cloud sampled from |ψ|². Rotatable 3D probability cloud."},
{"1*", "Atom Lab is born", "Nucleus with one electron on a Bohr orbit."},
